What’s the Big Deal? The Canon 5DS R offers lots of resolution. It outputs a print size just short of 20″ × 30″ @ 300 dpi, making it a commercial and landscape photographer’s dream. At this resolution, it’s a direct competitor to digital medium format.

50.6MP Full-Frame Sensor. At the heart of the 5DS R is a 50.6MP full-frame sensor that enables shallow depth of field. Its pixel pitch is essentially the same as the 7D Mark II’s, so optimistically high ISO performance should be “similar.” Please note that the normal ISO range has been limited to 100-6400 with the ability to “expand” to 12,800. The 5DS R’s dual DIGIC 6 image processors move all that data around, and yet they still provide speedy performance with up to 5FPS in continuous shooting.

61-Point High-Density Reticular AF. The autofocus system is basically the 5D Mark III’s, with 61 total autofocus points, 41 of which are cross-type. It also features the EOS iTR (Intelligent Tracking and Resolution), which uses the metering sensor for tracking, but the big difference is the addition of the 150,000-pixel RGB metering sensor from the 7D. This enhances tracking and continuous focus performance, breathing new life into the “old” 5D III’s system.

HD Video. While the 5DS R shoots video, that’s not its focus. It offers full HD 1080p capture at 30FPS in either ALL-I and IPB compressions, just as the 5D III did; however, the camera loses uncompressed HDMI output and the headphone jack for monitoring.

Built-In Intervalometer and Bulb Timer. The built-in intervalometer and bulb timer enable in-camera time-lapse and bulb settings without the need for an advanced remote shutter release. You can also set it to output an HD video file of the captured time-lapse.

Looking for a File-Transfer Solution? If you’re looking for a wired and wireless file-transfer solution, check out the Canon WFT-E7A Wireless Transmitter.', 4150000.0, '{"Brand": "Canon", "Camera Mount": "Canon EF", "Camera Type": "DSLR", "Compatible Media": "CF and SD", "Flash": "Type \\t\\tExternal only \\tMaximum Sync Speed \\t\\t1/200 Second \\tFlash Compensation \\t\\t-3 EV to +3 EV (in 1/3 or 1/2 EV steps) \\tFlash System \\t\\tDedicated: eTTL \\t\\tConnection: Hot Shoe, PC Terminal", "Imaging": "Maximum Resolution \\t\\tApproximately 50.3 MP \\t\\t3 × 2: 8688 × 5792 16 × 9: 1920 × 1080 \\tSensor \\t\\tType: CMOS \\t\\tSize: 36 × 24mm \\tCrop Factor \\t\\tx1.0 \\tBit Depth \\t\\tStills: 14-bit Video: 8-bit 4:2:0 \\tISO \\t\\tDynamic Range: Approximately 12.4 Stops \\t\\tDefault Sensitivity Range: 100-6400 \\t\\tExpanded Sensitivity Range: 50-12,800 \\t\\tNative ISO: 100 \\tLPF \\t\\tLow-pass filter effect cancellation", "Inputs/Outputs": "Audio Input \\t\\tMic: 3.5 mm (⅛”) stereo mini-jack \\tAudio Output \\t\\tNone \\tVideo Outputs \\t\\tHDMI Type C (mini HDMI) \\tAdditional Inputs/Outputs \\t\\tRemote: Canon N3-Type USB 3.0", "Item Type": "Camera", "Maximum Frame Rate": "30", "Maximum Photo Resolution": "53.0 MP", "Maximum Video Resolution": "1080p", "Monitoring": "Viewfinder \\t\\tPentaprism \\t\\t100% Coverage \\tLCD Panel \\t\\t3.2” Rear LCD \\t\\tButton Controls \\tInternal Microphone \\tStereo", "Physical": "Measurements \\t\\t6.0 × 4.6 × 3.0″ / 15.2 × 11.7 × 7.6 cm \\t\\t1.89lbs / 857.29g \\tAccessory Mounts: \\t\\tTripod: 1x ¼-20”  \\t\\tAdditional: Hot shoe", "Power Supply": "Battery GripBG-E11 Battery \\t\\tType: LP-E6/LP-E6N \\t\\tOperating Time: up to 900 shots or approximately 100 minutes of video \\t\\tRecharge Time: about 2.5 hours (with included LC-E6N)", "Recording": "Media \\t\\tCF: UDMA 7 SDHC/SDXC: UHS-I  UHS-II not supported  \\tHigh Resolution Card Requirements \\t\\tCF: 30 MB/s or faster \\t\\tSD: 20 MB/s or faster    Formats \\t\\tPhot